Chapter: TerrainRender
  cites Terrain chapter TerrainModel
  cites Clouds chapter CloudModel
  cites Water chapter OceanModel
  cites Foreword chapter Console

 Raymarches the height field into an image on the GOP framebuffer.

 A height field is marched rather than rasterised because the model is a
 FUNCTION of x and z, not a mesh. The ocean renderer builds a grid and fills
 triangles because it needs the surface at controlled positions; here the
 surface can be asked about anywhere, and marching gives an exact silhouette
 along every ridge line instead of one quantised to a grid.

 Traced at half resolution and written as 2x2 blocks, for the reason
 `CloudRender` gives.

Section: Framebuffer

  tr-fb-base : Integer
  tr-fb-base = #BF000000

  tr-width : Integer
  tr-width = 640

  tr-height : Integer
  tr-height = 400

  tr-tw : Integer
  tr-tw = 320

  tr-th : Integer
  tr-th = 200

  tr-half-w : Real
  tr-half-w = 160.0

  tr-half-h : Real
  tr-half-h = 100.0

  tr-focal : Real
  tr-focal = 250.0

  tr-pack : Real, Real, Real -> Integer
  tr-pack (r) (g) (b) =
    let ri = real-to-int (w-sat r * 255.0 + 0.5)
    in let gi = real-to-int (w-sat g * 255.0 + 0.5)
    in let bi = real-to-int (w-sat b * 255.0 + 0.5)
    in ri * 65536 + gi * 256 + bi

  tr-put : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  tr-put (x) (y) (color) =
    if x < 0 then 0
    else if y < 0 then 0
    else if x >= tr-width then 0
    else if y >= tr-height then 0
    else poke-32 tr-fb-base ((y * tr-width + x) * 4) color

 Cleared to magenta first so a capture that lands mid-draw is obvious
 rather than plausible. See the note in `CloudRender`; it was written after
 a partial frame was mistaken for a finished one.

  tr-clear-color : Integer
  tr-clear-color = #FF00FF

  tr-clear-row : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  tr-clear-row (x) (y) =
    if x >= tr-width then 0
    else let d = tr-put x y tr-clear-color
    in tr-clear-row (x + 1) y

  tr-clear : Integer -> Integer
  tr-clear (y) =
    if y >= tr-height then 0
    else let d = tr-clear-row 0 y
    in tr-clear (y + 1)

  tr-put-block : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  tr-put-block (tx) (ty) (color) =
    let x = tx * 2
    in let y = ty * 2
    in let a = tr-put x y color
    in let b = tr-put (x + 1) y color
    in let c = tr-put x (y + 1) color
    in tr-put (x + 1) (y + 1) color

Section: Camera

  Cam = record { cf : V3, cr : V3, cu : V3 }

  tr-cross : V3, V3 -> V3
  tr-cross (a) (b) =
    V3 {
      x = a.y * b.z - a.z * b.y,
      y = a.z * b.x - a.x * b.z,
      z = a.x * b.y - a.y * b.x
    }

  tr-eye : V3
  tr-eye = V3 { x = 0.0, y = 330.0, z = 0.0 }

  tr-world-up : V3
  tr-world-up = V3 { x = 0.0, y = 1.0, z = 0.0 }

  tr-forward : V3
  tr-forward = V3 { x = 0.22, y = 0.0 - 0.13, z = 0.97 }

  tr-camera : Cam
  tr-camera =
    let f = v-norm tr-forward
    in let r = v-norm (tr-cross tr-world-up f)
    in Cam { cf = f, cr = r, cu = tr-cross f r }

  tr-ray : Cam, Integer, Integer -> V3
  tr-ray (cam) (tx) (ty) =
    let sx = (real-from-int tx - tr-half-w + 0.5) / tr-focal
    in let sy = (tr-half-h - real-from-int ty - 0.5) / tr-focal
    in let a = v-add (cam.cf) (v-scale (cam.cr) sx)
    in v-norm (v-add a (v-scale (cam.cu) sy))

Section: The March

 A linear march with a GROWING step. Uniform steps small enough to catch a
 ridge close to the camera are far more than are needed a kilometre out,
 where one step already covers less than a pixel; growing the step spends
 the samples where the silhouette actually is. It is the same reasoning as
 the lengthening stride in the cloud model's light march.

 The march finds the first step whose end is under the terrain, which
 brackets the surface between two samples but locates it only to within a
 step. Bisecting that bracket is what makes the silhouette clean: without
 it the ridge lines are visibly quantised into the step size, and no
 affordable step count hides that.

  tr-under : V3, V3, Real -> Boolean
  tr-under (origin) (dir) (t) =
    let p = v-add origin (v-scale dir t)
    in p.y < t-height (p.x) (p.z)

  tr-refine : V3, V3, Real, Real, Integer -> Real
  tr-refine (origin) (dir) (lo) (hi) (iters) =
    if iters <= 0 then hi
    else let mid = (lo + hi) * 0.5
    in if tr-under origin dir mid then tr-refine origin dir lo mid (iters - 1)
    else tr-refine origin dir mid hi (iters - 1)

 The step grows but is CAPPED. Bisection locates the surface inside the
 bracket it is given and is only correct if the ray crosses the surface
 once inside it; uncapped growth reaches a step near 180 m by the
 hundredth sample while the finest octave of the field is around 39 m, so
 one step can straddle a ridge and the bisection then converges
 confidently to the wrong crossing. The cap has to stay below the smallest
 feature the field can produce, which makes it a property of
 `terrain-freq` and the octave count rather than a quality dial.

 **This cap is NOT what fixed the terracing, and it was blamed for it.**
 The first render came out in stair-step contours, the bracket argument
 above was the obvious explanation, the cap was added, and the terraces
 did not move at all. The cause was in the material blend, not here; see
 the note on `terrain-splat-scale` in `TerrainModel`, and the pair of
 experiments that cornered it. Rendering with a constant albedo is what
 cleared this file: the surface came out smooth, so the march and the
 normals were never the problem.

 The cap stays, because the reasoning for it is sound on its own terms and
 the bracket really can hold two crossings. The note stays because the
 same reasoning was sound for an artifact it was innocent of, and a
 mechanism that plausibly explains the wrong thing is more expensive than
 no explanation at all -- it stops the search.

  tr-max-step : Real
  tr-max-step = 24.0

  tr-march : V3, V3, Real, Real, Integer -> Real
  tr-march (origin) (dir) (t) (step) (steps) =
    if steps <= 0 then 0.0 - 1.0
    else if t > tr-max-dist then 0.0 - 1.0
    else let t2 = t + step
    in if tr-under origin dir t2 then tr-refine origin dir t t2 tr-refine-iters
    else let grown = step * tr-step-growth
    in let next = if grown > tr-max-step then tr-max-step else grown
    in tr-march origin dir t2 next (steps - 1)

  tr-max-dist : Real
  tr-max-dist = 5000.0

  tr-first-step : Real
  tr-first-step = 6.0

  tr-step-growth : Real
  tr-step-growth = 1.035

  tr-march-steps : Integer
  tr-march-steps = 260

  tr-refine-iters : Integer
  tr-refine-iters = 12

Section: Sky and Distance

  tr-sky : V3 -> V3
  tr-sky (dir) =
    let t = w-sat (dir.y * 2.0 + 0.08)
    in let base = v-lerp tr-horizon-color terrain-sky-color t
    in let s = w-sat (v-dot dir (v-norm terrain-sun))
    in let disc = w-powi s 64
    in let halo = w-powi s 8 * 0.20
    in v-add base (v-scale terrain-sun-color (disc + halo))

  tr-horizon-color : V3
  tr-horizon-color = V3 { x = 0.74, y = 0.80, z = 0.86 }

 Aerial perspective. Distant ground is not merely dimmer, it is closer to
 the colour of the air in front of it, and without this the far ridges read
 as being the same distance as the near ones.

  tr-fog-start : Real
  tr-fog-start = 500.0

  tr-fog-end : Real
  tr-fog-end = 4600.0

  tr-fog : V3, V3, Real -> V3
  tr-fog (color) (dir) (dist) =
    let f = c-remap dist tr-fog-start tr-fog-end 0.0 1.0
    in v-lerp color (tr-sky dir) (f * f)

Section: The Frame

  tr-sample : Cam, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  tr-sample (cam) (tx) (ty) =
    let dir = tr-ray cam tx ty
    in let hit = tr-march tr-eye dir 0.0 tr-first-step tr-march-steps
    in if hit < 0.0 then (let s = tr-sky dir in tr-pack (s.x) (s.y) (s.z))
    else let p = v-add tr-eye (v-scale dir hit)
    in let n = t-normal (p.x) (p.z)
    in let lit = t-shade p n
    in let c = tr-fog lit dir hit
    in tr-pack (c.x) (c.y) (c.z)

  tr-row : Cam, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  tr-row (cam) (tx) (ty) =
    if tx >= tr-tw then 0
    else let c = tr-put-block tx ty (tr-sample cam tx ty)
    in tr-row cam (tx + 1) ty

  tr-rows : Cam, Integer -> Integer
  tr-rows (cam) (ty) =
    if ty >= tr-th then 0
    else let d = tr-row cam 0 ty
    in tr-rows cam (ty + 1)

Section: Holding the Frame

 `-screenshot` captures at `-screenshot-delay` and ends the run there, so
 the guest must still be alive with a finished frame when the timer fires.
 Too short and the capture lands mid-draw; too long and no file is written
 at all. The hold is deliberately generous so the delay need not be exact.

  tr-tick-addr : Integer
  tr-tick-addr = 28672

  tr-hold-ticks : Integer
  tr-hold-ticks = 220

  tr-hold-fuel : Integer
  tr-hold-fuel = 40000000000

  tr-hold : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  tr-hold (until) (fuel) =
    if fuel <= 0 then 0
    else if peek-32 tr-tick-addr 0 >= until then 1
    else tr-hold until (fuel - 1)

  tr-hold-frame : Integer
  tr-hold-frame = tr-hold (peek-32 tr-tick-addr 0 + tr-hold-ticks) tr-hold-fuel

Section: Driver

  opening : [Console] Nothing
  opening = act
    print-line-uni "Terrain render (raymarched height field, triplanar, height-blended splat)"
    print-line-uni ("resolution = " & show tr-width & "x" & show tr-height & ", traced at " & show tr-tw & "x" & show tr-th)
    print-line-uni ("march steps = " & show tr-march-steps & ", refine = " & show tr-refine-iters)
    print-line-uni ("amplitude = " & show terrain-amplitude & ", sharpness = " & show terrain-tri-sharpness)
    let cam = tr-camera
    in let c0 = tr-clear 0
    in let d = tr-rows cam 0
    in act
      print-line-uni "drawn"
      print-line-uni ("held = " & show tr-hold-frame)
    end
  end
